      "message": {
        "content": "{\n  \"status\": \"unmet\",\n  \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n  \"useful_material\": \"该综述在'Effects of antibiotics'部分提及多项临床研究评估了抗生素暴露对 ICI 疗效的影响。其中一项纳入 249 例患者的研究指出，使用抗生素（包括β-内酰胺抑制剂、氟喹诺酮类或大环内酯类）在 PD-1/PD-L1 阻断治疗前 2 个月或后 1 个月内与较短的 PFS 和 OS 显著相关，但该研究将不同类别抗生素合并分析而非相互比较。另有两项回顾性研究分别显示抗生素暴露与 RCC 和 NSCLC 患者 ICI 疗效下降相关，但同样未区分抗生素类别。作者明确指出，在最大规模队列中，抗生素使用与 ICI 抵抗之间的关联是'一般预后关联还是因果联系'尚不明确。\",\n  \"auxiliary_material\": \"该综述提供了肠道微生物组影响 ICI 疗效的机制框架，包括抗生素通过破坏菌群多样性、减少有益菌属（如 Akkermansia muciniphila、Faecalibacterium、Bifidobacterium 等）丰度，进而影响 DC 成熟、T 细胞活化和细胞因子分泌。文中还讨论了研究间的不一致性来源，包括测序技术差异（16S rRNA vs 宏基因组）、样本采集方法、患者队列异质性、肿瘤类型和治疗方案不同等混杂因素。Table 3 列出了正在进行的临床试验，其中一项涉及哌拉西林 - 他唑巴坦或头孢吡肟对 Clostridiales 丰度的影响，但未涉及 ICI 疗效的类别比较。\",\n  \"remaining_gap\": \"该材料未能满足成功标准：(1) 未找到至少 2 项直接比较不同抗生素类别（如β-内酰胺类 vs 氟喹诺酮类）对 ICI 疗效影响差异的研究——文中提及的研究均将多种抗生素类别合并为'抗生素暴露'变量进行分析；(2) 作者未明确声明现有材料仅支持'广谱抗生素'整体效应，仅指出研究间存在不一致性且因果关系尚不明确。因此，关于特定抗生素类别是否相对安全的问题在此材料中保持未决状态。\",\n  \"outline_action\": \"建议继续检索：(1) 专门比较不同抗生素类别对 ICI 疗效影响的原始研究或荟萃分析；(2) 2019 年之后发表的更新综述，可能包含更多类别特异性数据；(3) 药物流行病学研究或真实世界证据，可能按抗生素类别分层分析 ICI 结局；(4) 若确认类别特异性数据确实缺乏，可在综述中明确说明当前证据仅支持'抗生素暴露'整体效应，并将此作为未来研究方向。\"\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to analyze a provided text (a review article about gut microbiome and immune checkpoint inhibitors) in relation to a specific gap question about antibiotic class specificity (beta-lactams vs fluoroquinolones) and ICI efficacy.\n\nI need to:\n1. Describe what this study (the review article) studied and found.\n2. Explain how this material may help with the requested gap.\n3. Assess the gap status (fulfilled, partial, unmet, unjudgeable).\n4. Assess material usefulness (direct, supporting, none).\n5. Identify useful_material and auxiliary_material.\n6. Describe remaining_gap and outline_action.\n\nKey constraints:\n- Do not call the gap fulfilled if success criteria are not met.\n- Success criteria: Find at least 2 studies comparing different antibiotic classes' effects on ICI efficacy, OR explicitly state that existing material only supports 'broad-spectrum antibiotics' overall effect.\n- Keep primary gap judgment separate from material usefulness.\n- Refer to the paper as 'this study' or 'the authors'.\n- Do not infer study design from silence.\n- Return one JSON object.\n\nLet me analyze the text for the gap question:\n\nGap question: Do different antibiotic classes (e.g., beta-lactams vs fluoroquinolones) have significantly different negative effects on ICI efficacy? Is there a specific class that is relatively safer?\n\nLooking at the text:\n\n1. In the \"Effects of antibiotics\" section:\n- One study of 249 patients mentions \"antibiotics (beta-lactam inhibitors, fluoroquinolones, or macrolides) 2 months before or 1 month after PD-1/PD-L1 blockade was significantly associated with shorter PFS and OS\" [20]. This mentions multiple classes but doesn't compare them against each other - it groups them together.\n\n- One retrospective study of 15 NSCLC patients found no significant difference in response/PFS between antibiotic-exposed and non-exposed groups [22].\n\n\n- Another retrospective study of RCC and NSCLC patients found worse outcomes with antibiotic exposure up to 30 days before checkpoint inhibitor [23].\n\n2. In the \"Immune-mediated colitis\" section:\n- One prospective cohort showed antibiotics before ipilimumab did not influence baseline dominant microbiota and none of the potentially predictive taxa were associated with antibiotic use [24].\n\n3. In the \"Translation from preclinical to clinical settings\" section:\n- The authors note inconsistencies across studies regarding antibiotic effects.\n- They mention that in the largest cohort investigating antibiotics' impact, factors like diet, country of origin, and other medications were not accounted for [23].\n- The authors state they are \"unclear whether this reflects a general prognostic association or a causative link with resistance to checkpoint inhibition.\"\n\n4. Critically, I don't find any direct comparison between different antibiotic classes (beta-lactams vs fluoroquinolones specifically). The text mentions multiple classes were used in studies but treats them as a grouped \"antibiotic exposure\" variable rather than comparing class-specific effects.\n\n5. In Table 3 (ongoing clinical studies), one study mentions \"Piperacillin–tazobactam or cefepime\" but this is about fold-change in Clostridiales abundance, not ICI efficacy comparison between classes.\n\nSuccess criteria assessment:\n- The gap asks for at least 2 studies comparing different antibiotic classes' effects on ICI efficacy\n- OR explicitly state that existing material only supports 'broad-spectrum antibiotics' overall effect\n\nThe text does mention that studies grouped antibiotics together (beta-lactam inhibitors, fluoroquinolones, or macrolides) but does NOT provide class-specific comparisons. The authors do not explicitly state that existing material only supports broad-spectrum antibiotics overall effect - they note inconsistencies but don't make this specific claim.\n\nSo the success criteria are NOT fully met:\n- No 2 studies comparing different antibiotic classes are identified in this text\n- The text does not explicitly state that existing material only supports 'broad-spectrum antibiotics' overall effect (though it implies this by grouping them)\n\nStatus should be \"partial\" or \"unmet\" - I think \"partial\" is appropriate because the material does provide relevant information about antibiotic effects on ICI, just not the class-specific comparison requested.\n\nActually, looking more carefully at the success criteria: \"找到至少 2 项比较不同抗生素类别对 ICI 疗效影响的研究，或明确说明现有材料仅支持'广谱抗生素'整体效应。\"\n\nThe text does NOT have 2 studies comparing different classes. Does it explicitly state that existing material only supports broad-spectrum antibiotics overall effect? The text mentions antibiotics were grouped together in studies but doesn't make an explicit statement that class-specific data doesn't exist. So this criterion is not met either.\n\nI should mark status as \"unmet\" because neither success criterion is satisfied. However, the material is still \"supporting\" because it provides context about antibiotic effects on ICI efficacy generally, discusses mechanisms, and notes inconsistencies in the literature.\n\nFor useful_material: The information about antibiotic exposure being associated with worse outcomes in some studies, the mention of specific antibiotic classes used (beta-lactam inhibitors, fluoroquinolones, macrolides), the discussion of inconsistencies across studies, and the mechanisms by which antibiotics might affect ICI efficacy through microbiome disruption.\n\nFor auxiliary_material: The discussion of microbiome composition associated with ICI response, the preclinical evidence, the ongoing clinical trials, the methodological considerations.\n\nFor remaining_gap: No class-specific comparison data is provided. The text groups antibiotics together rather than comparing beta-lactams vs fluoroquinolones specifically.\n\nFor outline_action: Need to search for primary studies that specifically compare antibiotic classes, or find more recent reviews/meta-analyses that address this specific question.\n\nLet me structure the JSON response now.",
